nat44-ed: hairpinning on a LAN that is not an inside interface

The NAT44-ED counterpart of 0043: "set interface nat44 hairpinning
<intfc> [del]" (and "show nat44 hairpinning interfaces") puts
nat44-ed-hairpin-classify on the interface after input ACLs, reassembly,
dns-intercept and ABF. TCP and UDP to the external address and port of a
static mapping (identity mappings excepted), and packets to a NAT address
that match an existing flow (the inside service's replies on a hairpinned
session, which no static mapping matches), go to nat44-ed-in2out, or to
nat44-in2out-worker-handoff with more than one worker; the rest continues
untouched.

The same address array as 0043 (pool addresses and non-identity static
mappings' external addresses) filters destinations first; it also keeps
ordinary outbound sessions, whose packets match flows too, out of the flow
lookup. ED's in2out rewrites both addresses of
a hairpinned session itself (with 0040 also the reply's source port).

nat44-ed-in2out continues to nat.arc_next, the next feature as seen from
the node that took it, so the classify node is a sibling of nat-default
and shares its next-node list; it sets arc_next the way nat-pre-in2out
does, and the handoff sets it itself. Registering it with its own next
nodes made in2out loop on a wrong index until the heap ran out.

The interface's FIB is counted for per-VRF sessions as an inside
interface's would be. Only the NAT plugin changes.

Verified in an isolated VPP as for 0043. Test:
tests/integration/integration_nat44_partial_checksum.py --nat ed --mode hairpin.

--- a/src/plugins/nat/nat44-ed/nat44_ed.c
